      "title": "Software Development Infrastructure in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iran's enterprise software, implementation partners, deployment models, and support services, with clear limits on the available evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "overview",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The business systems behind an organization",
        "content": "Software infrastructure includes the applications through which an organization records transactions, manages work, and maintains information. In the Iranian supplier material inspected here, these functions extend across finance, personnel, purchasing, production, and administrative correspondence. System Group, known in Persian as Hamkaran System, presents enterprise resource planning, business intelligence, and business process management among its offerings. Enterprise resource planning, usually abbreviated to ERP, connects operational functions; business intelligence organizes information for analysis; business process management concerns the design and execution of workflows. These are supplier descriptions of intended capabilities, rather than independent measurements of results. [s1]\n\nThis distinction helps explain the scope of the directory. An enterprise software company supplies a product or product family. A service company helps an organization introduce, configure, or maintain technology. Financial and administrative software describes the functions being supported. Custom development and architecture consulting concern how particular requirements are translated into software and system design. These are editorial distinctions for navigating the subject: a single supplier can operate across several of them.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "deployment",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Deployment changes the division of responsibility",
        "content": "The same product can be offered through different operating arrangements. System Group's Rahkaran documentation distinguishes a licensed, customer-hosted model from a cloud subscription. Its comparison assigns infrastructure, information security, and updates to the customer in the former arrangement, and to System Group in the latter. This establishes the vendor's stated allocation of responsibilities; it does not independently establish security performance or uninterrupted availability. [s2]\n\nFor international readers, an important distinction follows: application functionality and operating responsibility are separate questions. An organization might select familiar accounting functions while changing who maintains the servers and installs updates. Conversely, a change of application need not entail a move to cloud hosting. The inspected product documentation supports examining these choices separately rather than treating cloud deployment as a synonym for enterprise software. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "delivery-network",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A product also needs a delivery network",
        "content": "System Group's partner directory can be filtered by geography, industry, and product area. It identifies different partner relationships, including specialist group companies and licensed firms. The directory therefore provides evidence of an organized implementation channel rather than a product-only business. Its project counters are not used here as market statistics: the inspected page does not provide the reporting periods and consistent definitions needed for an annual comparison. [s3]\n\nIndustry specialization is another part of delivery. Dana System describes an ERP implementation practice focused on oil and gas, including upstream and downstream activities, and presents localization as part of its work. Its account illustrates how a service provider can organize expertise around industrial processes. Assertions about rapid implementation, low cost, or successful outcomes remain company claims and are not adopted as independently established findings. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "organizational-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Implementation extends beyond installation",
        "content": "Historical research provides a useful counterweight to product marketing. The inspected abstract of a study published in Management Decision examines Iranian ERP adopters and emphasizes management support, communication, and user training. It also discloses that implementation success was measured through subjective perceptions because factual organizational data were difficult to obtain. The findings support attention to organizational preparation, but cannot establish today's national success rate or rank current suppliers. [s5]\n\nTaken together, the evidence supports a practical reading of software infrastructure: applications, deployment arrangements, implementation expertise, and continuing organizational participation are connected. It does not yet support a comprehensive account of Iran's custom development businesses, architecture consultancies, or regional distribution of technical capacity. Those areas require further research before this overview can represent the whole subfield.",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3", "s4", "s5"]
      }
